<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Graz</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Graz is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and the capital of the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>, on the Mur river. It grew around a castle hill in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and became the capital of Styria and, from 1564 to 1619, the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> archdukes of Inner Austria, who directed the defence of the Croatian and Slavonian Military Frontier from there. Its old town and Eggenberg Palace are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/graz.jpg" alt="Graz" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nick Savchenko from Kiev, Ukraine · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Graz_(35932179023).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Graz lies on both sides of the river Mur in southeast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, about 150 kilometres southwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and some 50 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maribor">Maribor</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>. It is in the Graz Basin on the eastern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, with the city centre at 353 metres, and the Schlossberg, or Castle Hill, 475 metres high, dominates the old town. Because the basin is open only to the south, the climate is warmer than expected at that latitude.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The oldest settlement on the site dates to the Copper Age, but there is no continuity of settlement before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. The name most likely derives from the Slavic gradec, small castle, though the city itself was founded by Bavarian settlers who arrived shortly after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> and intermixed with them. They called it Bayrischgraz, Bavarian Graz, to distinguish it from the older Windischgraz, Slovenian Graz, which it soon eclipsed. The name first appears in records in 1128, and the earliest documented mention dates from the 12th century, when Graz developed as a fortified settlement under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-babenberg">Babenbergs</a>.</p><p>In the Late Middle Ages Graz became a commercial and administrative centre and the residence of the Inner Austrian branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg dynasty</a>, from which come many of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> buildings. It was also a stronghold against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, through the fortifications on the Schlossberg. As capital of the multiethnic Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> it was a centre of Slovene culture from the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-graz">University of Graz</a> in 1586 until the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ljubljana">Ljubljana</a> was established in 1919. Industrialisation in the 19th and 20th centuries made it a major industrial and cultural centre of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Graz is the capital of the Austrian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> and the second-largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, with 307,912 residents with a primary residence. Its historic centre, with over 1,000 buildings from the Gothic to the contemporary, was added to the UNESCO World Heritage List in 1999, and Eggenberg Palace on the western edge of the city in 2010. Graz was European Capital of Culture in 2003, and the Clock Tower on the Schlossberg is its symbol.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Graz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The finds from the Burgstallkogel are mostly in the Landesmuseum Joanneum at <b>Graz</b>, which also holds the Strettweg Cult Wagon.</blockquote><a href="/culture/hallstatt-culture">Hallstatt culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Styria was governed by its dukes with strong estates meeting in the Landhaus at <b>Graz</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-styria">Duchy of Styria</a></li><li><blockquote>These duchies were ruled as Inner Austria by Leopold's descendants in the 15th century; Frederick III, who resided at <b>Graz</b> and Wiener Neustadt, later reunited the dynasty's lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/inner-austria">Inner Austria</a></li><li><blockquote>The Otakar margraves gradually made the march independent of Carinthia, adding lands around <b>Graz</b> and in the Enns valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-styria">Margraviate of Styria</a></li><li><blockquote>The frontier was run by army officers answering to the War Council in Vienna and <b>Graz</b>, not to the Croatian ban or Hungarian diet.</blockquote><a href="/state/military-frontier">Military Frontier</a></li></ul></section>
